RevivedHack does have a debug mode. When you specify -D on the command
line, the game gives you the name "wizard" and sets a flag that is
stored in saved games. A debug player can choose his/her character,
but always starts out with a wand of 20 wishes, and reincarnates after
dying (at least in most cases). I don't mention this in the
documentation mostly because for me this really spoils the game unless
you're tracking down a bug.

I almost didn't do the "BSOD" screen (which can be quickly dispatched
with a keypress, and doesn't come up _that_ often--mostly when
changing levels), but then I thought there really should be some
additional discouragement. I assumed that players of Roguelikes would
have already heard of savescumming, or at least have some
understanding of "permadeath". I have personally found no need for it
in debugging. I think the -D switch is useful enough for that. I play
this game quite frequently, and have felt the temptation numerous
times to back up a game that's going well. The extra trouble in
cheating does help to discourage me, at least.

In a future version I will probably add a warning message before a
duplicate saved game is restored, so the player has a chance of
changing his/her mind. I may also get rid of the "BSOD". I don't know,
it just seemed funny at the time ;-)

